\par\b\fs32 Documentation \u8212? AI Content Forensics System\b0\fs22\par
\par
This document summarizes architecture, chosen libraries, setup & run steps, calibration instructions, testing, and the final submission checklist.\par
\par
\par\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\par
\par
\par\b\fs28 1. Project Overview\b0\fs22\par
\par
\par\tab• Purpose: Detect whether a text is AI\u8209?generated and provide explainable evidence (feature contributions and token highlights).\par
\par\tab• Audience: Hackathon judges, reviewers, and researchers who need a fast, interpretable detector.\par
\par
\par\b\fs28 2. Architecture (high level)\b0\fs22\par
\par
\par\tab• UI: `app.py` \u8212? Streamlit wide layout, sidebar controls, single/batch input modes, and a detailed result panel. Uses `src/token_highlighter.py` for token highlights.\par
\par\tab• Feature extraction: `src/feature_extractor.py` \u8212? computes interpretable features (avg word length, type/token ratio, punctuation ratio, stopword ratio, repeat word fraction, word_count).\par
\par\tab• Heuristic detector: `src/detector.py` \u8212? maps features to a heuristic probability via weighted sum + sigmoid.\par
\par\tab• Classifier: `src/classifier.py` \u8212? trains/loads either a sklearn TF\u8209?IDF + LogisticRegression pipeline (`models/model.joblib`) or falls back to `src/simple_classifier.py` which is a pure\u8209?Python Naive Bayes saved at `models/simple_model.json`.\par
\par\tab• Explainer: `src/explainer.py` \u8212? computes feature contributions and token contributions for explainability.\par
\par\tab• Batch: `scripts/batch_server.py` \u8212? lightweight HTTP endpoint for programmatic predictions (POST `/predict`).\par
\par
\par\b\fs28 3. Key files\b0\fs22\par
\par
\par\tab• `app.py` \u8212? Streamlit application (entry point).\par
\par\tab• `scripts/train_model.py` \u8212? train / calibrate script. Use `--calibrate path/to/labeled.csv`.\par
\par\tab• `run_demo.py` \u8212? CLI demo that saves `demo_output.json`.\par
\par\tab• `models/` \u8212? saved models (`model.joblib` for sklearn, `simple_model.json` for fallback).\par
\par\tab• `wheels/` \u8212? optional offline wheel files for Windows Python 3.13 (use `pip install --no-index --find-links=...`).\par
\par\tab• `ROMAN_URDU_README.md` \u8212? Roman\u8209?Urdu explainer for judges; printable via `scripts/markdown_to_pdf.py`.\par
\par
\par\b\fs28 4. Setup & Run (Windows example)\b0\fs22\par
\par
1. Create & activate venv (PowerShell):\par
\par\f1\fs18
python -m venv .venv-1\par
& ".venv-1\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1"\par
\par\f0\fs22
2. Install dependencies (online):\par
\par\f1\fs18
python -m pip install --upgrade pip\par
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt\par
\par\f0\fs22
3. Run Streamlit app:\par
\par\f1\fs18
& ".venv-1\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1"\par
python -m streamlit run app.py\par
\par\f0\fs22
4. Train / calibrate model on labeled CSV (optional):\par
\par\f1\fs18
python scripts\\train_model.py --calibrate path\\to\\labeled.csv\par
\par\f0\fs22
\par
Offline wheels: if network unstable, download wheel files on another machine and install locally:\par
\par\f1\fs18
python -m pip install --no-index --find-links="C:\\path\\to\\wheels" scikit-learn scipy joblib\par
\par\f0\fs22
\par
\par\b\fs28 5. Improving accuracy (practical steps)\b0\fs22\par
\par
1. Collect representative labeled data (20\u8211?200 samples per class minimum). CSV format: `text,label` where `label` is `0` (human) or `1` (ai).\par
2. Run `python scripts\\train_model.py --calibrate labeled.csv` to retrain sklearn pipeline (if scikit-learn installed) or update fallback model via `train_from_dataset()`.\par
3. Evaluate with a held-out test set and adjust `ml_weight` or ensemble threshold.\par
4. Optional: augment features (readability scores, POS tag distributions, perplexity from small LM) for greater robustness.\par
\par
\par\b\fs28 6. Testing & QA\b0\fs22\par
\par
\par\tab• Unit tests: run `python -m pytest -q` (tests located in `tests/`).\par
\par\tab• Quick sanity: `python scripts\\run_sample_analysis.py` to reproduce sample scores.\par
\par
